// A METHOD-GENERIC RECEIVER LOST ITS LOAN, SO TWO LIVE `&mut` INTO THE SAME
// FIELD WERE ADMITTED. The one variable against its twin — and against
// pass/bc_genrecv_two_mut_sequential_admit — is the METHOD'S TYPE PARAMETER,
// nothing else:
//     fn plain(&mut self) -> &mut i64      c.plain(); c.plain();   refused
//     fn get<T>(&mut self) -> &mut i64     c.get::<i64>(); x2      ADMITTED
// Byte-identical bodies, byte-identical call sites, opposite verdicts.
//
// THE ROOT. mono rewrites a method-generic call into a plain `Code::Call` with
// the receiver autoref'd into arg0. take_ref_borrows' Call arm ties arg0 to the
// holder only when the first formal is a `DstRef` — the FAT receiver case — so
// a receiver typed plain `&mut C` deposited nothing and record_borrow was never
// reached. The MethodCall arm (which does tie, via method_result_borrows_self)
// no longer sees the node: there is no MethodCall left after mono.
//
// ⚠ THE CORPUS COULD NOT PRICE THIS. Measured 2026-08-27 with
// scripts/pass-probe.sh over 8518 pass/fail tests plus the stdlib: the FINDING
// column (400 rustc-rejected `tests/imported/admit` rows, a channel proven live
// — the sabotage pole moves all 400) came back ZERO. This program had to be
// CONSTRUCTED. The census's 2328 arrivals at the fall-through are arrivals, not
// defects, and the acceptance ledger contains no program of this shape.
// ⚠ A SIBLING SUB-POPULATION IS STILL OPEN, AND IT IS NAMED HERE RATHER THAN
// LEFT TO BE REDISCOVERED. The fix this file pins covers a METHOD-generic call.
// A method on a GENERIC STRUCT is admitted still, with no type parameter on the
// method at all:
//     struct C<T> { v: T }
//     impl<T> C<T> { fn get(&mut self) -> &mut T { return &mut self.v; } }
//     let a = c.get(); let b = c.get(); *a = 2; *b = 3;   // ADMITTED
// MEASURED, not assumed: that program reaches NEITHER the tie above NOR the
// genautoref observer NOR the genarg0blind insurance — zero fires on all three
// — so it is not this site under-firing, it is a different path, and widening
// here cannot reach it. Its non-generic twin (`struct C { v: i64 }`, same
// method) refuses. It gets its own round and its own measurement.

package bc_genrecv_two_mut_conflict_fail;

struct C { v: i64 }

impl C {
    fn get<T>(&mut self) -> &mut i64 { return &mut self.v; }
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut c: C = C { v: 1i64 };
    let a: &mut i64 = c.get::<i64>();
    let b: &mut i64 = c.get::<i64>();
    *a = 2i64;
    *b = 3i64;
    return 0i32;
}
